pipeline of snapshots

A producer that must run in order (a simulation, time depending on
time) and consumers that don't care about order (rasterizing,
compressing) are decoupled by one copy: the producer emits a compact
snapshot of its state each step and immediately moves on. Workers take
snapshots in any order; results are reassembled by sequence number at
the end.

The copy looks like waste and is the whole trick — it is the fence
that lets the sequential heart beat at full speed while the
parallelizable limbs swing freely. Seen before in: double-buffered
renderers, WAL-shipping databases, film pipelines (frames to render
farm). Used here: particle sim → frame snapshots → splatting + LZW
workers → gif assembly.

Corollary: design the snapshot to be small and flat (numbers, not
references) and the pattern pays for itself even single-threaded,
because it draws a clean testable border through the middle of the
machine.
